d  at over 5,000 New Zealanders with an  average age of 65 some participants were  given a monthly high dose of vitamin D  the others got a placebo they were  followed for an average of just over  three years researchers wanted to see if  the vitamin D supplement helped prevent  cardiovascular disease to her what did  they find so there's been a lot of  questions around the relationship  between vitamin D and cardiovascular  house since the 1980s when they started  to make observations that people with  low vitamin D seemed to be an increased  risk of cardiovascular disease but the  question really has been to  supplementation actually help and in  this study when they looked at the  groups who got the vitamin D and those  who didn't both groups had about the  same risk twelve percent of developing  cardiovascular disease down the line the  vitamin D group was able to increase  their vitamin D levels but again it  seemed like in this study with monthly  high dose supplementation  there really was no benefit and the  authors are quick to point out though we  don't know what would happen with  different doses with different  frequencies so daily or weekly  supplementation or longer follow ups  there's still a lot of unanswered  questions how popular is vitamin D and  vitamin D supplements in the US well  with supplements in general are about  half of the population takes about 1 30  so you know a lot of people take it  there's a lot of data about this really  hard to show that the supplements really  do help they're still going they're  doing research steve nison from the  Cleveland Clinic emailed me and told me  that he's doing a big study 13,000  people looking at omega 3 fatty acids to  see if it has a role in people with high  triglycerides and low HDL low good  cholesterol but right now he said I'm  not he said I'm not surprised by this  study that I never did drink the  kool-aid kool-aid being vitamin D that's  what he said Tara's a cardiologist do a  lot of your patients ask about  supplements for heart health they do and  they ask about fish oil and things like  that but I think what's more important  is that what people don't tell and  whether they don't tell because they're  afraid that their doctor will say don't  take it or they don't think it's  important but it is important because a  lot of these supplements there really is  no proven benefit to them for most  healthy Americans things like multi  vitamins and antioxidants and for other  things like herbals you know these are  really a class that is unregulated they  don't have to prove safety efficacy or  quality before they get out of the  market they can be mislabeled they can  be contaminated they can have stimulants  in them and they can interact with a  patient's regular prescription drug so  just because it's natural doesn't mean  it's safe talk to your doctor about what  you're on okay with that doctors Jon  LaPook and Tara narula great to see you  thanks for your time
 

